# Career Vision Statement Activity

This activity guides you through a structured writing process to develop a meaningful Career Vision Statement. You will build it in layers — starting with raw material from your previous work, developing a draft, refining it, and then committing to a final version.

---

## Step 1 — Gather Your Raw Material

Before writing, pull together the following from your previous work:

- Your Personal Empathy Map insights
- Your SWOT Analysis
- Your Personal POV Statement
- Your RIASEC Profile
- Your Career Exploration Map

---

## Step 2 — Answer the Foundation Questions

**What kind of work do I want to do?**
*(Think about tasks, not job titles. What activities do you want to fill your professional days with? What problems do you want to work on?)*
I would want to be able to turn my brain off while I do tasks, and work from home. I do not mean mindless working, but only use that part of the brain that lets you clean while you listen to music. I also want to be able to take walking breaks after doing some work and pet my cat, so working from home would be really good. I also need a lot of money from my job to be happy.

**Who do I want to serve or impact?**
*(Who are the people, communities, or sectors your work will benefit? Be as specific as you can.)*
I think with the job I want to do I will realistically be working with older folk who have newer electronics, but do not know how to actually avoid scams. I will also likely be working with businesses who want to train all their workers o how to not get viruses on the work computers. Even though I will just be watching and making sure nothing goes wrong, I will also most likely have to give other people cybersecurity information.

**What values do I want my career to embody?**
The values that I want my career to embody is helping others move forward alongside the future of technology.

**What kind of professional do I want to become?**
*(What qualities, character traits, or reputation do you want to be known for in your field?)*
I want to be known for qualities or character traits such as smart, easy to come to for help, a nice person, not a high achiever, and loving towards animals.

**What does success look like for me — not financially, but meaningfully?**
*(Set salary aside for a moment. What would make you feel that your career was truly worthwhile?)*
What would make my career wothrwhile is being able to work while strugeling with adhd- like accomidations while I work. I want to be able to stay at home with a really nice house. I want to be able to live comfortably, and be able to have hobbies.

---

## Step 3 — Draft Your Career Vision Statement

Using your foundation questions as raw material, write a first draft of your Career Vision Statement. Aim for 3–5 sentences. Use the structure below as a guide — but feel free to depart from it once you have a draft:

> "My career vision is to [type of work] for/with [who you serve], so that [impact or outcome you want to create]. I want to be known as a professional who [qualities/values]. I see myself building a career in [field or sector] where I can [specific contribution], grounded in my commitment to [core value]."

**My Draft Career Vision Statement:**
My career vision is to able to turn my brain off while I do tasks, and work from home for/with old folks and companies needing help learning about internet safety, so that everyone can move into the future evolving alongside the evolving internet. I want to be known as a professional who is smart, is easy to come to for help, a nice person, not a high achiever, and who loves animals. I see myself building a career for a bank or corporation where I can work from home and be accomidated for my ADHD, grounded in my commitment to live comfortably at a nice home where I can pet my cat.

---

## Step 4 — Refine and Finalize

Read your draft aloud (or read it slowly in your head). Ask yourself:

- Does this sound like me, or does it sound like what I think I should say?
- Is it specific enough to be meaningful?
- Is it broad enough to allow for growth?
- Does it excite me when I read it?

Revise as needed and write your final version below.

**My Final Career Vision Statement:**

> My career vision is to be able to turn my brain off while I do tasks, and work from home for/with old folks and companies needing help learning about internet safety, so that everyone can move into the future evolving alongside the evolving internet. I want to be known as a professional who is smart, is easy to come to for help, a nice person, not a high achiever, and who loves animals. I see myself building a career for a bank or corporation where I can work from home and be accommodated for my ADHD, grounded in my commitment to live comfortably at a nice home where I can pet my cat. 

---

💡 **Note:** Your Career Vision Statement is a living document. You are not locked into it forever. You will revisit and revise it in Module 5 and beyond as your self-knowledge deepens and your career exploration continues.
